  <p>The 80% rule is provisional data completeness guidance, not a psychometric or clinical cutoff. NA excludes items only with reasons; NO and unanswered items remain in the applicable denominator. No total, norm or risk category is calculated.</p>
  <h2>Professional report — Approved</h2><main class="report-body"><h1>Educational Observation Report</h1>
<h2>Source and scope</h2>
<p>This report is based solely on one parent&#39;s (father&#39;s) observations in the home setting. It describes Chinese literacy and learning experiences reported at home for a Grade 9 student named Nancy. No other respondent&#39;s record, school assessment, or external data has been included, inferred, or compared. Item ratings and domain means are descriptive observations from this respondent only; they are not norm-referenced, diagnostic, validated screening, risk, or clinical severity scores.</p>
<h2>Context</h2>
<ul><li>Grade: 9. Observation period: 2026-08-03 to 2026-10-12. Contact frequency: several times a week.</li><li>Home language: Chinese (Mandarin). Observation language: Chinese. Script: simplified.</li><li>Respondent reported no school suspension; primary-school Chinese grades were described as &quot;middle-to-lower.&quot;</li><li>Respondent noted that Chinese has not been assessed in the past 2 years.</li><li>Respondent described the student as working independently and reported reading confidence as having &quot;some limits&quot; and writing confidence as &quot;comfortable.&quot;</li><li>Respondent-reported reasons for concern: difficulty using words accurately and broadly (in both writing and oral expression); difficulty distinguishing main, secondary, and non-essential points; difficulty consistently narrating events in correct order; reluctance to express ideas spontaneously; limited flexibility with wording; and repeated errors on the same characters.</li><li>Respondent-reported strengths: 4 years of Chinese classics study, able to recite some classical texts, but unable to apply this to writing.</li><li>Respondent reported no such family activities. Respondent described the home setting as involving frequent parental conflict and asked how to help Nancy with trauma from an adverse family environment and how to encourage confidence under disadvantage.</li><li>School feedback (source: parent): writing ability and word use are relatively weak.</li><li>Respondent estimated current reading/writing level at Grade 5 and considered the foundation weak, stating this is &quot;unrelated to intelligence.&quot;</li><li>Respondent reported impact level 3 across all domains.</li></ul>
<section class="domain-results"><h2>Observations by Domain</h2><section class="domain-card"><h3>Reading</h3>
      <figure class="rating-chart" role="img" aria-label="Reading item rating distribution. 1 Never or almost never: 3; 2 Occasionally: 2; 3 Sometimes: 4; 4 Often: 1; 5 Almost always: 0">
        <figcaption>Distribution of observed-frequency ratings · number of items</figcaption><div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-label">1 · Never or almost never</span><span class="bar-track"><span class="bar-fill" style="width:75%"></span></span><span class="bar-count">3</span></div><div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-label">2 · Occasionally</span><span class="bar-track"><span class="bar-fill" style="width:50%"></span></span><span class="bar-count">2</span></div><div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-label">3 · Sometimes</span><span class="bar-track"><span class="bar-fill" style="width:100%"></span></span><span class="bar-count">4</span></div><div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-label">4 · Often</span><span class="bar-track"><span class="bar-fill" style="width:25%"></span></span><span class="bar-count">1</span></div><div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-label">5 · Almost always</span><span class="bar-track"><span class="bar-fill" style="width:0%"></span></span><span class="bar-count">0</span></div>
        
      </figure>
      <p class="domain-narrative">10 applicable items received numeric ratings. Rating counts (1–5): 1: 3, 2: 2, 3: 4, 4: 1, 5: 0. The most common rating was 3 (sometimes). The descriptive mean is 2.3 of 5. Ratings of 4 or 5 were recorded on 1 item (P-R1); this is a review aid, not a cutoff. Rated-item coverage was 100% of applicable items. Not observed: 0; not applicable: 0; unanswered: 0. These categories are kept distinct and are not treated as negative ratings. Separately recorded access impact: 3 of 3; impact is not averaged into the frequency ratings.</p>
      <div class="interpretation"><h4>Interpretation</h4><p>The response pattern is centered on 3 (Sometimes). Ratings of Often or Almost always on 1 item identify specific experiences to review in context. The pattern does not establish cause, diagnosis, or clinical severity. Separately, the respondent marked access impact as “Substantial restriction of participation or demonstration of learning”; this describes reported participation or demonstration of learning and is not part of the frequency mean.</p></div>
      <h4>Items to review in support planning</h4><ul><li><code>P-R1</code> — Understands similar Chinese content more successfully when someone reads it aloud than when reading it independently. <strong>(4/5 · Often)</strong></li><li><code>P-R3</code> — Has difficulty explaining the meaning of a Chinese sentence they have read independently. <strong>(3/5 · Sometimes)</strong></li><li><code>P-R4</code> — Has difficulty explaining the main idea and important details in an appropriate Chinese passage. <strong>(3/5 · Sometimes)</strong></li><li><code>P-R5</code> — Misreads familiar Chinese characters or words when reading aloud at home. <strong>(3/5 · Sometimes)</strong></li><li><code>P-R6</code> — Frequently pauses or stops reading because characters or words are not recognized. <strong>(3/5 · Sometimes)</strong></li></ul>
      <div class="planning-recommendation"><h4>Recommendation for support planning</h4><p>In the Support plan tab, review P-R1, P-R3, P-R4, P-R5, P-R6 and any linked examples. If staff confirm an access barrier, connect a priority to those observations and define a suitable strategy, owner, observable progress indicator, and review date.</p></div>
      <h4>Items rated often or almost always</h4><ul><li><code>P-R1</code> — Understands similar Chinese content more successfully when someone reads it aloud than when reading it independently. <strong>(4/5)</strong></li></ul><p><strong>Reported strengths:</strong> 1.需要良好稳定的家庭气氛和鼓励更容易成功。2.画画和体育有一点优势</p><p><strong>Current supports:</strong> 1.每周3-4次在校大学生兼职辅导作业。2.每周3-4次（每次25分钟）网上一对一口语对话</p>
      <p class="caution">Rating counts include numeric responses only. Not observed, not applicable, and unanswered responses are reported separately; no norm or diagnostic cutoff is applied.</p>
