      <p class="domain-narrative">7 applicable items received numeric ratings. Rating counts (1–5): 1: 5, 2: 2, 3: 0, 4: 0, 5: 0. The most common rating was 1 (never or almost never). The descriptive mean is 1.3 of 5. No rated items were at 4 or 5; this is a description of this record, not a rule-out. Rated-item coverage was 100% of applicable items. Not observed: 0; not applicable: 0; unanswered: 0. These categories are kept distinct and are not treated as negative ratings. Separately recorded access impact: 3 of 3; impact is not averaged into the frequency ratings.</p>
      <div class="interpretation"><h4>Interpretation</h4><p>The response pattern is centered on 1 (Never or almost never). Ratings are concentrated below Sometimes in this record. This indicates less frequent observations here, but does not rule out needs in other tasks or settings. Separately, the respondent marked access impact as “Substantial restriction of participation or demonstration of learning”; this describes reported participation or demonstration of learning and is not part of the frequency mean.</p></div>
      
      <div class="planning-recommendation"><h4>Recommendation for support planning</h4><p>No item was rated Sometimes or more often, but the separately recorded impact or examples may warrant contextual discussion. Review them in the Support plan tab; if staff confirm a barrier, document a context-specific priority rather than dismissing it based on frequency ratings.</p></div>
      <p><strong>Reported strengths:</strong> 开心时能够较好完成</p><p><strong>Current supports:</strong> 没有支持</p>
      <p class="caution">Rating counts include numeric responses only. Not observed, not applicable, and unanswered responses are reported separately; no norm or diagnostic cutoff is applied.</p>
    </section></section>
<h2>Strengths and current supports</h2>
<ul><li>Respondent-reported strengths: 4 years (elsewhere stated 3–4 years) of Chinese classics study; able to recite some classical texts; some strength in drawing and sports; can complete motor tasks relatively well when happy.</li><li>Respondent reported that Nancy responds better with a stable, encouraging family atmosphere and that slow communication in a good family atmosphere works well.</li><li>Current supports reported: 3–4 times weekly homework help from a university student tutor; 3–4 times weekly 25-minute online one-to-one oral conversation; the father helps explain some sentences Nancy cannot understand and some logical connections between sentences.</li><li>Respondent priorities: (1) oral expression; (2) applying learned knowledge in writing; (3) combining words and sentences; (4) understanding new words and sentences. Owner listed: 刘晓航. Related items: P-R3, P-W5, P-SA1. Indicator: able to express a medium-complexity event completely and clearly. Review date: 2026-11-30. Strategy reference: compound-meaning. Respondent noted &quot;good family atmosphere and slow communication work very well.&quot;</li></ul>
<h2>Limitations and points for review</h2>
<ul><li>Instrument limitation (as supplied): This developmental parent observation profile describes Chinese literacy and learning experiences reported in the home setting. It supports educational planning. It does not establish or exclude dyslexia or another diagnosis, and its scores are not compared with population norms. Parent and teacher versions have not been established as equivalent measures.</li><li>This report reflects one parent&#39;s home observations only. Chinese has not been assessed in the past 2 years per the respondent. The respondent estimated a Grade 5 reading/writing level and described the foundation as weak, while stating this is unrelated to intelligence.</li><li>The respondent described an adverse family environment (frequent parental conflict) as a significant context and asked about supporting trauma and confidence; these are matters for appropriate professional review, not conclusions of this report.</li><li>The spatial and visual domain has insufficient observations (one item rated, one not applicable); no domain mean was calculated. &quot;Not applicable&quot; and &quot;not observed&quot; responses should not be interpreted as absence of difficulty.</li><li>Points for professional discussion (drawn from the respondent&#39;s own reported priorities and concerns): oral expression; applying learned knowledge in writing; word and sentence combination; understanding new words and sentences; the reported gap between oral discussion and written output; and the reported impact of family atmosphere on learning.</li></ul>
<h2>Recommended monitoring plan</h2>
<p>During the agreed support period, monitor observable changes in Nancy&#39;s Chinese literacy and learning engagement at home using the same parent-observation format. Track whether she more often explains the meaning of sentences she reads independently, organizes ideas more clearly in written responses, and uses practised words or phrases in new writing, as these were identified priorities. Also note changes in her willingness to participate in shared reading or writing activities, her frustration during difficult tasks, and her use of the existing at-home supports (tutoring and online oral practice), without assigning school teaching duties to the parent. Review these observations at a mutually agreed interval after support begins, with the exact timing and any additional indicators to be confirmed during professional review.</p>
<h2>Recommended follow-up plan</h2>
<p>A repeat home observation may be helpful to describe any changes in the same domains after support has been in place, and around 6–8 weeks after support starts may be considered as a suitable time frame. This repeat observation should remain optional and use the same parent respondent, home setting, and applicable domains (Reading, Written expression, Chinese literacy patterns, Sequencing and organization, Oral language, and Learning experience and wellbeing). Because questions remain about trauma, confidence, and lack of recent Chinese assessment, further assessment by qualified professionals is recommended in parallel and should not be delayed while support is provided. Any changes noted in a repeat observation would be descriptive only and should be interpreted within professional review.</p><section class="report-next-steps"><h2>Recommendations and next steps</h2>
    <p>These observations inform a separate, staff-reviewed support plan. After this report is approved, the plan is developed and approved separately before it is included in the final combined report. Parent and teacher evidence remains separate.</p>
  </section></main>
  <h2>Support plan</h2><p><strong>Pathway:</strong> additional information needed</p>
       <p><strong>Status:</strong> Agreed · 2026-10-13T07:43:56.541Z</p>
       <p><strong>Rationale:</strong> This record contains only one parent&#39;s home observations for a Grade 9 student, with no school-setting information or recent Chinese assessment. The respondent reports home concerns in word use, written expression, sequencing, oral expression, and literacy-related avoidance, and also raises trauma and confidence questions. These are matters for qualified professional review, and school-setting information is needed before a classroom-focused pathway can be considered. Support at home may proceed in parallel while further information is gathered.</p><ul><li>At home, during shared reading of accessible Chinese text, preview key vocabulary and use read-aloud or audio access where appropriate so Nancy can focus on meaning rather than effortful decoding; follow with brief supported reading and feedback. (P-R1, P-R3, P-R6; strategy reading-access). Owner parent or caregiver; progress Nancy more often explains the meaning of a sentence she has read with support, and pauses less often because characters are not recognized.; review .</li><li>For home writing tasks, use oral planning first, then models and organizers, sentence construction practice, and revision routines; allow an appropriate alternate response mode when written output is effortful. (P-W5, P-GP3, P-GP4; strategy written-organization). Owner parent or caregiver; progress Nancy more often organizes ideas clearly in a short written response and shows in writing more of what she can already discuss aloud.; review .</li><li>Use predictable support, private rehearsal, meaningful choice, and help-seeking routines around Chinese reading and writing at home to reduce avoidance and frustration. (P-SA1, P-SA2; strategy literacy-frustration). Owner parent or caregiver; progress Nancy more often participates in shared reading or writing activities at home and less often delays or avoids difficult Chinese tasks.; review .</li></ul>
  
  <section><h2>Reading</h2><p>Strengths: 1.需要良好稳定的家庭气氛和鼓励更容易成功。2.画画和体育有一点优势</p><p>Supports: 1.每周3-4次在校大学生兼职辅导作业。2.每周3-4次（每次25分钟）网上一对一口语对话</p><p>Home setting: 有固定专用书桌; observation language: 英语.中文.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Exact observation wording</th><th>Response / reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-R1</td><td>Understands similar Chinese content more successfully when someone reads it aloud than when reading it independently.</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>P-R2</td><td>Has difficulty recognizing Chinese characters or words that they have learned or regularly encounter.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>P-R3</td><td>Has difficulty explaining the meaning of a Chinese sentence they have read independently.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-R4</td><td>Has difficulty explaining the main idea and important details in an appropriate Chinese passage.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-R5</td><td>Misreads familiar Chinese characters or words when reading aloud at home.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-R6</td><td>Frequently pauses or stops reading because characters or words are not recognized.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-R7</td><td>Has difficulty using punctuation or sentence boundaries to support phrasing and meaning when reading aloud.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-R8</td><td>Confuses familiar similar-sounding Chinese characters or words when reading, after considering the spoken variety used.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-R9</td><td>Has difficulty reading Chinese materials that have already been taught or practised.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>P-R10</td><td>Has difficulty finding reading-related mistakes in their work after being shown how to check.</td><td>1</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section><h2>Dictation and transcription</h2><p>Strengths: 加强训练</p><p>Supports: 很少</p><p>Home setting: 父母频繁争吵给娃娃带来巨大影响; observation language: 中文.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Exact observation wording</th><th>Response / reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-D1</td><td>Leaves out or incorrectly writes practised Chinese characters or words when they are spoken aloud during ordinary homework.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-D2</td><td>Makes character or word errors when copying Chinese text from a clear nearby model.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-D3</td><td>Needs substantial time to copy a short familiar Chinese text accurately.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>P-D4</td><td>Loses their place or leaves out parts when copying Chinese text from a screen, book, or another model.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-D5</td><td>Writes the same practised Chinese character or word differently across similar attempts.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section><h2>Written expression</h2><p>Strengths: 加强日常沟通并且缓慢进行</p><p>Supports: 正在加强父母的自我反思中</p><p>Home setting: 基础家教严重缺失; observation language: 中文.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Exact observation wording</th><th>Response / reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-W1</td><td>Can say or explain a familiar Chinese word but has difficulty remembering its written characters.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-W2</td><td>Expresses ideas less fully in Chinese writing than when discussing a similar topic aloud.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-W3</td><td>Has difficulty using punctuation to make Chinese written meaning clear after it has been taught or practised.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-W4</td><td>Has difficulty putting familiar Chinese words or phrases together into a meaningful written sentence.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-W5</td><td>Has difficulty arranging ideas clearly in a Chinese written response.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-W6</td><td>Uses Chinese sentence structures that make the intended written meaning difficult to understand, after considering language exposure.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-W7</td><td>Leaves out, adds, or changes the order of Chinese characters or words in ways that change the intended written meaning.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section><h2>Chinese literacy patterns</h2><p>Strengths: 加强基础知识积累</p><p>Supports: 正在进行中</p><p>Home setting: 家庭氛围不好情况下更明显; observation language: 普通话.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Exact observation wording</th><th>Response / reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-CL1</td><td>Confuses familiar Chinese characters that contain similar-looking parts or arrangements.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-CL2</td><td>Leaves out, changes, or misplaces parts when writing familiar Chinese characters.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-CL3</td><td>Has difficulty using a taught meaning-related character part to help explain a character, when this strategy is familiar.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-CL4</td><td>Has difficulty using a taught sound-related character part as a pronunciation clue when the clue is useful.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-CL5</td><td>Has difficulty explaining familiar meaningful parts of a compound Chinese word after this has been taught or practised.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-CL6</td><td>Has difficulty using sentence meaning to distinguish familiar Chinese words that sound the same.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-CL7</td><td>Has difficulty using familiar word parts and context to suggest the meaning of a new compound Chinese word.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-CL8</td><td>Takes substantial time to recall the names of familiar Chinese characters or words during ordinary reading.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-CL9</td><td>Has difficulty recognizing the same learned Chinese characters in familiar print or digital fonts.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>P-CL10</td><td>Has difficulty grouping Chinese characters into meaningful words or phrases when reading a passage.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-CL11</td><td>Has difficulty remembering newly practised Chinese characters across later opportunities at home.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-CL12</td><td>Needs substantial help to use a practised Chinese character or word in a new sentence or piece of writing.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section><h2>General performance</h2><p>Strengths: 提高阅读量</p><p>Supports: 因时间不足没有进行</p><p>Home setting: 家庭氛围一般情况下; observation language: 普通话.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Exact observation wording</th><th>Response / reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-GP1</td><td>Repeatedly erases or rewrites during Chinese homework because producing or checking the written response is difficult.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-GP2</td><td>Shows noticeably different performance across similar Chinese reading or writing activities at home.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-GP3</td><td>Can discuss a topic more successfully than show the same understanding in a written response at home.</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>P-GP4</td><td>Has difficulty showing what they have practised when doing a similar written activity at home.</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>P-GP5</td><td>Has difficulty recalling how to write practised Chinese characters or words when they are spoken aloud.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-GP6</td><td>Needs substantial time or effort to complete a familiar Chinese reading or writing activity at home.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section><h2>Attention and task engagement</h2><p>Strengths: 需要专业的意见指导</p><p>Supports: 很少</p><p>Home setting: 同样的道理使用范围很有限; observation language: 中文..</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Exact observation wording</th><th>Response / reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-CN1</td><td>Loses focus on a home learning activity when attention shifts to other things.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-CN2</td><td>Has difficulty staying engaged long enough to complete an accessible home learning activity.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-CN3</td><td>Loses recently presented information before using it, despite first showing understanding.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section><h2>Memory for learning</h2><p>Strengths: 改善家庭氛围</p><p>Supports: 调整过程中</p><p>Home setting: 家庭氛围不好和一般情况下; observation language: 普通话.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Exact observation wording</th><th>Response / reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-MM1</td><td>Has difficulty remembering recently practised information during home learning.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-MM2</td><td>Loses parts of an instruction with several steps after first showing that they understand it.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-MM3</td><td>Needs an understood home learning instruction or message repeated in order to remember it.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-MM4</td><td>Has difficulty recalling familiar names or vocabulary when needed.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-MM5</td><td>Has difficulty remembering a short meaningful spoken sequence after supported practice.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-MM6</td><td>Has difficulty remembering relevant homework deadlines or a familiar schedule without reminders.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-MM7</td><td>Has difficulty recalling previously learned information needed for a current home activity.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section><h2>Sequencing and organization</h2><p>Strengths: 耐心指导</p><p>Supports: 很少</p><p>Home setting: 家庭氛围一般去看下; observation language: 中文.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Exact observation wording</th><th>Response / reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-SQ1</td><td>Has difficulty forming practised Chinese characters in a workable stroke sequence during handwriting.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-SQ2</td><td>Has difficulty organizing dates or deadlines in a familiar format that has been explained.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-SQ3</td><td>Has difficulty understanding time relationships such as before, after, earlier, or later during a relevant activity.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-SQ4</td><td>Has difficulty recounting events or following a familiar procedure in a coherent order.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-SQ5</td><td>Has difficulty estimating or planning the time needed for a home learning activity.</td><td>3</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section><h2>Oral language</h2><p>Strengths: 改善家庭氛围耐心交流</p><p>Supports: 很少</p><p>Home setting: 一般情况下; observation language: 中文.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Exact observation wording</th><th>Response / reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-L1</td><td>Needs additional time to understand and respond during conversation in a familiar home language.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-L2</td><td>Has difficulty giving a clear connected explanation in a familiar home language.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-L3</td><td>Has difficulty finding words that communicate the intended meaning in a familiar home language.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-L4</td><td>Produces speech that is difficult to understand after considering accent, dialect, language exposure, and context.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section><h2>Learning experience and wellbeing</h2><p>Strengths: 改善家庭氛围，补上基础知识.习惯</p><p>Supports: 没有</p><p>Home setting: 一般情况下; observation language: 中文.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Exact observation wording</th><th>Response / reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-SA1</td><td>Delays or avoids Chinese reading or writing activities that they appear to find difficult.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-SA2</td><td>Has difficulty participating comfortably in shared reading, writing, or homework activities at home.</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>P-SA3</td><td>Says negative things about their ability to learn, read, or write.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-SA4</td><td>Becomes frustrated or stops participating when Chinese literacy difficulty occurs.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-SA5</td><td>Reports or shows physical discomfort around stressful home learning activities.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section><h2>Mathematics access</h2><p>Strengths: 加强基础知识的牢固</p><p>Supports: 暂时不需要</p><p>Home setting: 一般情况下; observation language: 普通话.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Exact observation wording</th><th>Response / reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-M1</td><td>Has difficulty choosing or using an effective method for a calculation that has been taught.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-M2</td><td>Has difficulty understanding the wording of a mathematics problem during homework.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-M3</td><td>Has difficulty explaining familiar taught number or place-value ideas.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>P-M4</td><td>Confuses the meaning or use of familiar mathematical symbols.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-M5</td><td>Has difficulty recalling practised arithmetic facts when an activity calls for them.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section><h2>Spatial and visual task access</h2><p>Strengths: 通过训练应该能完成</p><p>Supports: 没有支持</p><p>Home setting: 一般情况下; observation language: 普通话.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Exact observation wording</th><th>Response / reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-SP1</td><td>Has difficulty using directions such as left, right, above, or below in relevant home activities.</td><td>not_applicable — 很少出现这种情况，只有精力不集中时会出现</td></tr><tr><td>P-SP2</td><td>Has difficulty keeping their place or locating information on an accessible page, screen, diagram, or workspace.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></section><section><h2>Motor access</h2><p>Strengths: 开心时能够较好完成</p><p>Supports: 没有支持</p><p>Home setting: 一般情况下; observation language: 中文.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Exact observation wording</th><th>Response / reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P-MC1</td><td>Has difficulty controlling handwriting enough to complete an accessible written activity.</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>P-MC2</td><td>Has difficulty coordinating movement during familiar everyday activities.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>P-MC3</td><td>Shows or reports tiredness or discomfort that limits participation in observed movement or hand-use activities.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>P-MC4</td><td>Has difficulty participating in familiar movement activities even when instructions and materials are suitable.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>P-MC5</td><td>Has difficulty handling objects needed for everyday activities.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>P-MC6</td><td>Has difficulty following a familiar movement sequence or a practised rhythmic pattern.</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>P-MC7</td><td>Has difficulty managing learning materials or age-relevant everyday tasks because of movement or hand-use demands.</td><td>2</td></tr></tbody></table></section><h2>Limitations</h2><p>This developmental parent observation profile describes Chinese literacy and learning experiences reported in the home setting. It supports educational planning. It does not establish or exclude dyslexia or another diagnosis, and its scores are not compared with population norms. Parent and teacher versions have not been established as equivalent measures.</p>
